R191	Cueing Arg1:T156 Arg2:T157	
T158	Quotation 29460 29475	"There, there,"
T159	Cue 29476 29480	said
T160	Entity 29481 29487	Kovrin
R192	Addressee Arg1:T160 Arg2:T157	
R193	Cueing Arg1:T159 Arg2:T158	
R194	Speaker Arg1:T160 Arg2:T158	
T161	Entity 29499 29502	her
R195	Addressee Arg1:T161 Arg2:T158	
T162	Quotation 29509 29678	"You've quarrelled with each other, you've cried, and that's enough. You must not be angry for long-- that's wrong . . . all the more as he loves you beyond everything."
R196	Speaker Arg1:T160 Arg2:T162	
R197	Addressee Arg1:T161 Arg2:T162	
R198	Cueing Arg1:T159 Arg2:T162	
T163	Quotation 29679 29720	"He has . . . has spoiled my whole life,"
T164	Entity 29721 29726	Tanya
T165	Cue 29727 29734	went on
R199	Speaker Arg1:T164 Arg2:T163	
R200	Cueing Arg1:T165 Arg2:T163	
R201	Addressee Arg1:T160 Arg2:T163	
T166	Quotation 29745 29935	"I hear nothing but abuse and . . . insults. He thinks I am of no use in the house. Well! He is right. I shall go away to-morrow; I shall become a telegraph clerk. . . . I don't care. . . ."
R202	Speaker Arg1:T164 Arg2:T166	
R203	Cueing Arg1:T165 Arg2:T166	
R204	Addressee Arg1:T160 Arg2:T166	
T167	Quotation 29936 30113	"Come, come, come. . . . You mustn't cry, Tanya. You mustn't, dear . . . . You are both hot-tempered and irritable, and you are both to blame. Come along; I will reconcile you."
R205	Addressee Arg1:T164 Arg2:T167	
T168	Entity 30114 30120	Kovrin
R206	Speaker Arg1:T168 Arg2:T167	
T169	Entity 31348 31351	She
T170	Cue 31352 31359	went on
T171	Quotation 31376 31447	complaining of her father and her hard, insufferable life in that house
T172	Entity 31460 31466	Kovrin
R207	Cueing Arg1:T170 Arg2:T171	
R208	Speaker Arg1:T169 Arg2:T171	
R209	Addressee Arg1:T172 Arg2:T171	
T173	Entity 31618 31621	she
T174	Cue 31622 31628	called
T175	Quotation 31637 31643	a fool
R210	Speaker Arg1:T173 Arg2:T175	
R211	Cueing Arg1:T174 Arg2:T175	
R212	Addressee Arg1:T172 Arg2:T175	
T176	Quotation 33059 33082	"But you are a mirage,"
T177	Cue 33083 33087	said
T178	Entity 33088 33094	Kovrin
R213	Cueing Arg1:T177 Arg2:T176	
R214	Speaker Arg1:T178 Arg2:T176	
T179	Quotation 33096 33171	"Why are you here and sitting still? That does not fit in with the legend."
R215	Cueing Arg1:T177 Arg2:T179	
R216	Speaker Arg1:T178 Arg2:T179	
T180	Quotation 33172 33195	"That does not matter,"
T181	Entity 33196 33204	the monk
R217	Addressee Arg1:T181 Arg2:T176	
R218	Addressee Arg1:T181 Arg2:T179	
R219	Speaker Arg1:T181 Arg2:T180	
T182	Cue 33205 33213	answered
T183	Entity 33271 33274	him
R220	Cueing Arg1:T182 Arg2:T180	
R221	Addressee Arg1:T183 Arg2:T180	
T184	Quotation 33276 33373	"The legend, the mirage, and I are all the products of your excited imagination. I am a phantom."
R222	Speaker Arg1:T181 Arg2:T184	
R223	Cueing Arg1:T182 Arg2:T184	
R224	Addressee Arg1:T183 Arg2:T184	
T185	Quotation 33374 33397	"Then you don't exist?"
T186	Cue 33398 33402	said
T187	Entity 33403 33409	Kovrin
R225	Cueing Arg1:T186 Arg2:T185	
R226	Speaker Arg1:T187 Arg2:T185	
T188	Quotation 33411 33439	"You can think as you like,"
T189	Cue 33440 33444	said
T190	Entity 33445 33453	the monk
R227	Addressee Arg1:T190 Arg2:T185	
R228	Speaker Arg1:T190 Arg2:T188	
R229	Cueing Arg1:T189 Arg2:T188	
R230	Addressee Arg1:T187 Arg2:T188	
T191	Quotation 33475 33567	"I exist in your imagination, and your imagination is part of nature, so I exist in nature."
R231	Cueing Arg1:T189 Arg2:T191	
R232	Speaker Arg1:T190 Arg2:T191	
R233	Addressee Arg1:T187 Arg2:T191	
T192	Quotation 33568 33686	"You have a very old, wise, and extremely expressive face, as though you really had lived more than a thousand years,"
T193	Cue 33687 33691	said
T194	Entity 33692 33698	Kovrin
R234	Cueing Arg1:T193 Arg2:T192	
R235	Speaker Arg1:T194 Arg2:T192	
R236	Addressee Arg1:T190 Arg2:T192	
T195	Quotation 33700 33840	"I did not know that my imagination was capable of creating such phenomena. But why do you look at me with such enthusiasm? Do you like me?"
R237	Cueing Arg1:T193 Arg2:T195	
R238	Speaker Arg1:T194 Arg2:T195	
R239	Addressee Arg1:T190 Arg2:T195	
T196	Quotation 33841 34176	"Yes, you are one of those few who are justly called the chosen of God. You do the service of eternal truth. Your thoughts, your designs, the marvellous studies you are engaged in, and all your life, bear the Divine, the heavenly stamp, seeing that they are consecrated to the rational and the beautiful-- that is, to what is eternal."
R240	Speaker Arg1:T190 Arg2:T196	
R241	Addressee Arg1:T194 Arg2:T196	
T197	Quotation 34177 34296	"You said 'eternal truth.' . . . But is eternal truth of use to man and within his reach, if there is no eternal life?"
R242	Speaker Arg1:T194 Arg2:T197	
T198	Quotation 34297 34321	"There is eternal life,"
T199	Cue 34322 34326	said
T200	Entity 34327 34335	the monk
R243	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T197	
R244	Cueing Arg1:T199 Arg2:T198	
R245	Speaker Arg1:T200 Arg2:T198	
R246	Addressee Arg1:T194 Arg2:T198	
T201	Quotation 34337 34380	"Do you believe in the immortality of man?"
R247	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T201	
R248	Cueing Arg1:T199 Arg2:T201	
R249	Speaker Arg1:T194 Arg2:T201	
T202	Quotation 34381 34948	"Yes, of course. A grand, brilliant future is in store for you men. And the more there are like you on earth, the sooner will this future be realised. Without you who serve the higher principle and live in full understanding and freedom, mankind would be of little account; developing in a natural way, it would have to wait a long time for the end of its earthly history. You will lead it some thousands of years earlier into the kingdom of eternal truth-- and therein lies your supreme service. You are the incarnation of the blessing of God, which rests upon men."
R250	Speaker Arg1:T200 Arg2:T202	
R251	Addressee Arg1:T194 Arg2:T202	
T203	Quotation 34949 34990	"And what is the object of eternal life?"
T204	Cue 34991 34996	asked
T205	Entity 34997 35003	Kovrin
R252	Cueing Arg1:T204 Arg2:T203	
R253	Speaker Arg1:T205 Arg2:T203	
R254	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T203	
T206	Quotation 35005 35230	"As of all life-- enjoyment. True enjoyment lies in knowledge, and eternal life provides innumerable and inexhaustible sources of knowledge, and in that sense it has been said: 'In My Father's house there are many mansions.'"
R255	Addressee Arg1:T205 Arg2:T206	
R256	Speaker Arg1:T200 Arg2:T206	
T207	Quotation 35231 35281	"If only you knew how pleasant it is to hear you!"
T208	Cue 35282 35286	said
T209	Entity 35287 35293	Kovrin
R257	Cueing Arg1:T208 Arg2:T207	
R258	Speaker Arg1:T209 Arg2:T207	
R259	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T207	
T210	Quotation 35332 35349	"I am very glad."
R260	Addressee Arg1:T209 Arg2:T210	
R261	Speaker Arg1:T200 Arg2:T210	
T211	Quotation 35350 35512	"But I know that when you go away I shall be worried by the question of your reality. You are a phantom, an hallucination. So I am mentally deranged, not normal?"
R262	Speaker Arg1:T209 Arg2:T211	
R263	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T211	
T212	Quotation 35513 35849	"What if you are? Why trouble yourself? You are ill because you have overworked and exhausted yourself, and that means that you have sacrificed your health to the idea, and the time is near at hand when you will give up life itself to it. What could be better? That is the goal towards which all divinely endowed, noble natures strive."
R264	Addressee Arg1:T209 Arg2:T212	
R265	Speaker Arg1:T200 Arg2:T212	
T213	Quotation 35850 35905	"If I know I am mentally affected, can I trust myself?"
R266	Speaker Arg1:T209 Arg2:T213	
R267	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T213	
T214	Quotation 35906 36309	"And are you sure that the men of genius, whom all men trust, did not see phantoms, too? The learned say now that genius is allied to madness. My friend, healthy and normal people are only the common herd. Reflections upon the neurasthenia of the age, nervous exhaustion and degeneracy, et cetera, can only seriously agitate those who place the object of life in the present-- that is, the common herd."
R268	Addressee Arg1:T209 Arg2:T214	
R269	Speaker Arg1:T200 Arg2:T214	
T215	Quotation 36310 36362	"The Romans used to say: Mens sana in corpore sano."
R270	Speaker Arg1:T209 Arg2:T215	
R271	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T215	
T216	Quotation 36363 36680	"Not everything the Greeks and the Romans said is true. Exaltation, enthusiasm, ecstasy-- all that distinguishes prophets, poets, martyrs for the idea, from the common folk-- is repellent to the animal side of man-- that is, his physical health. I repeat, if you want to be healthy and normal, go to the common herd."
R272	Speaker Arg1:T200 Arg2:T216	
T217	Quotation 36681 36737	"Strange that you repeat what often comes into my mind,"
T218	Cue 36738 36742	said
T219	Entity 36743 36749	Kovrin
R273	Addressee Arg1:T219 Arg2:T216	
R274	Cueing Arg1:T218 Arg2:T217	
R275	Speaker Arg1:T219 Arg2:T217	
R276	Addressee Arg1:T200 Arg2:T217	
T220	Quotation 36751 36884	"It is as though you had seen and overheard my secret thoughts. But don't let us talk about me. What do you mean by 'eternal truth'?"
T221	Entity 36885 36893	The monk
R277	Speaker Arg1:T219 Arg2:T220	
R278	Cueing Arg1:T218 Arg2:T220	
R279	Addressee Arg1:T221 Arg2:T220	
T222	Quotation 37138 37166	"The hallucination is over,"
T223	Cue 37167 37171	said
T224	Entity 37172 37178	Kovrin
T225	Quotation 37196 37210	"It's a pity."
R280	Cueing Arg1:T223 Arg2:T222	
R281	Cueing Arg1:T223 Arg2:T225	
R282	Speaker Arg1:T224 Arg2:T225	
T226	Quotation 38032 38047	"Are you here?"
T227	Entity 38048 38051	she
T228	Cue 38052 38056	said
R283	Speaker Arg1:T227 Arg2:T226	
R284	Cueing Arg1:T228 Arg2:T226	
T229	Entity 37766 37768	He
R285	Addressee Arg1:T229 Arg2:T226	
T230	Quotation 38058 38144	"And we have been looking and looking for you. . . . But what is the matter with you?"
T231	Entity 38145 38148	she
T232	Cue 38149 38154	asked
R286	Speaker Arg1:T231 Arg2:T230	
R287	Cueing Arg1:T232 Arg2:T230	
R288	Addressee Arg1:T229 Arg2:T230	
T233	Quotation 38229 38262	"How strange you are, Andryusha!"
R289	Speaker Arg1:T231 Arg2:T233	
R290	Addressee Arg1:T229 Arg2:T233	
T234	Quotation 38263 38285	"I am pleased, Tanya,"
T235	Cue 38286 38290	said
T236	Entity 38291 38297	Kovrin
T237	Entity 38318 38321	her
R291	Cueing Arg1:T235 Arg2:T234	
R292	Speaker Arg1:T236 Arg2:T234	
R293	Addressee Arg1:T237 Arg2:T234	
T238	Quotation 38333 38473	"I am more than pleased: I am happy. Tanya, darling Tanya, you are an extraordinary, nice creature. Dear Tanya, I am so glad, I am so glad!"
R294	Speaker Arg1:T236 Arg2:T238	
R295	Addressee Arg1:T237 Arg2:T238	
T239	Quotation 38522 38912	"I have just passed through an exalted, wonderful, unearthly moment. But I can't tell you all about it or you would call me mad and not believe me. Let us talk of you. Dear, delightful Tanya! I love you, and am used to loving you. To have you near me, to meet you a dozen times a day, has become a necessity of my existence; I don't know how I shall get on without you when I go back home."
T240	Entity 38474 38476	He
R296	Speaker Arg1:T240 Arg2:T239	
T241	Quotation 38913 38918	"Oh,"
T242	Cue 38513 38520	went on
R297	Cueing Arg1:T242 Arg2:T239	
T243	Cue 38919 38926	laughed
T244	Entity 38927 38932	Tanya
T245	Quotation 38934 39019	"you will forget about us in two days. We are humble people and you are a great man."
R298	Cueing Arg1:T243 Arg2:T241	
R299	Cueing Arg1:T243 Arg2:T245	
R300	Speaker Arg1:T244 Arg2:T241	
R301	Speaker Arg1:T244 Arg2:T245	
R302	Addressee Arg1:T244 Arg2:T239	
T246	Quotation 39020 39049	"No; let us talk in earnest!"
T247	Entity 39050 39052	he
T248	Cue 39053 39057	said
R303	Addressee Arg1:T247 Arg2:T241	
R304	Addressee Arg1:T247 Arg2:T245	
R305	Speaker Arg1:T247 Arg2:T246	
R306	Cueing Arg1:T248 Arg2:T246	
T249	Quotation 39059 39139	"I shall take you with me, Tanya. Yes? Will you come with me? Will you be mine?"
R307	Addressee Arg1:T244 Arg2:T246	
R308	Speaker Arg1:T247 Arg2:T249	
R309	Cueing Arg1:T248 Arg2:T249	
R310	Addressee Arg1:T244 Arg2:T249	
T250	Quotation 39140 39147	"Come,"
T251	Cue 39148 39152	said
T252	Entity 39153 39158	Tanya
T253	Quotation 39360 39418	"I was not thinking of it . . . I was not thinking of it,"
R311	Cueing Arg1:T251 Arg2:T250	
R312	Speaker Arg1:T252 Arg2:T250	
R313	Addressee Arg1:T247 Arg2:T250	
T254	Entity 39419 39422	she
T255	Cue 39423 39427	said
R314	Speaker Arg1:T254 Arg2:T253	
R315	Cueing Arg1:T255 Arg2:T253	
R316	Addressee Arg1:T247 Arg2:T253	
T256	Entity 39464 39470	Kovrin
T257	Entity 39480 39483	her
T258	Cue 39488 39503	went on talking
T259	Quotation 39547 39664	"I want a love that will dominate me altogether; and that love only you, Tanya, can give me. I am happy! I am happy!"
R317	Cueing Arg1:T258 Arg2:T259	
R318	Addressee Arg1:T257 Arg2:T259	
R319	Speaker Arg1:T256 Arg2:T259	
T260	Quotation 39823 39843	"How lovely she is!"
T261	Entity 39765 39767	he
T262	Entity 39776 39779	her
R320	Speaker Arg1:T261 Arg2:T260	
R321	Addressee Arg1:T262 Arg2:T260	
T263	Cue 43632 43641	muttering
T264	Entity 43510 43512	he
T265	Quotation 43643 44000	"Say what you like, there is a great deal in blood. His mother was a wonderful woman, most high-minded and intelligent. It was a pleasure to look at her good, candid, pure face; it was like the face of an angel. She drew splendidly, wrote verses, spoke five foreign languages, sang. . . . Poor thing! she died of consumption. The Kingdom of Heaven be hers."
R322	Cueing Arg1:T263 Arg2:T265	
R323	Speaker Arg1:T264 Arg2:T265	
T266	Entity 44012 44028	Yegor Semyonitch
T267	Cue 44055 44062	went on
T268	Quotation 44064 44471	"When he was a boy and growing up in my house, he had the same angelic face, good and candid. The way he looks and talks and moves is as soft and elegant as his mother's. And his intellect! We were always struck with his intelligence. To be sure, it's not for nothing he's a Master of Arts! It's not for nothing! And wait a bit, Ivan Karlovitch, what will he be in ten years' time? He will be far above us!"
R324	Cueing Arg1:T267 Arg2:T268	
R325	Speaker Arg1:T266 Arg2:T268	
T269	Entity 44499 44515	Yegor Semyonitch
T270	Cue 44599 44602	cry
T271	Quotation 44604 44748	"The devils! They have spoilt everything! They have ruined everything! They have spoilt everything! The garden's done for, the garden's ruined!"
R326	Cueing Arg1:T270 Arg2:T271	
R327	Speaker Arg1:T269 Arg2:T271	
T272	Quotation 47271 47286	"Good-morning,"
T273	Cue 47287 47291	said
T274	Entity 47292 47300	the monk
R328	Cueing Arg1:T273 Arg2:T272	
R329	Speaker Arg1:T274 Arg2:T272	
T275	Entity 47211 47213	he
R330	Addressee Arg1:T275 Arg2:T272	
T276	Entity 47326 47328	he
T277	Cue 47329 47334	asked
T278	Quotation 47336 47367	"What are you thinking of now?"
R331	Cueing Arg1:T277 Arg2:T278	
R332	Speaker Arg1:T276 Arg2:T278	
R333	Addressee Arg1:T275 Arg2:T278	
T279	Quotation 47368 47378	"Of fame,"
T280	Cue 47379 47387	answered
T281	Entity 47388 47394	Kovrin
R334	Cueing Arg1:T280 Arg2:T279	
R335	Speaker Arg1:T281 Arg2:T279	
R336	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T279	
T282	Quotation 47396 47584	"In the French novel I have just been reading, there is a description of a young savant, who does silly things and pines away through worrying about fame. I can't understand such anxiety."
R337	Cueing Arg1:T280 Arg2:T282	
R338	Speaker Arg1:T281 Arg2:T282	
R339	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T282	
T283	Quotation 47585 47707	"Because you are wise. Your attitude towards fame is one of indifference, as towards a toy which no longer interests you."
R340	Addressee Arg1:T281 Arg2:T283	
R341	Speaker Arg1:T276 Arg2:T283	
T284	Quotation 47708 47728	"Yes, that is true."
R342	Speaker Arg1:T281 Arg2:T284	
R343	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T284	
T285	Quotation 47729 48025	"Renown does not allure you now. What is there flattering, amusing, or edifying in their carving your name on a tombstone, then time rubbing off the inscription together with the gilding? Moreover, happily there are too many of you for the weak memory of mankind to be able to retain your names."
R344	Speaker Arg1:T281 Arg2:T285	
R345	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T285	
T286	Quotation 48026 48038	"Of course,"
T287	Cue 48039 48047	assented
T288	Entity 48048 48054	Kovrin
R346	Cueing Arg1:T287 Arg2:T286	
R347	Speaker Arg1:T288 Arg2:T286	
T289	Quotation 48056 48179	"Besides, why should they be remembered? But let us talk of something else. Of happiness, for instance. What is happiness?"
R348	Cueing Arg1:T287 Arg2:T289	
R349	Speaker Arg1:T288 Arg2:T289	
R350	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T286	
R351	Addressee Arg1:T274 Arg2:T289	
T290	Quotation 48289 48826	"In ancient times a happy man grew at last frightened of his happiness --it was so great!-- and to propitiate the gods he brought as a sacrifice his favourite ring. Do you know, I, too, like Polykrates, begin to be uneasy of my happiness. It seems strange to me that from morning to night I feel nothing but joy; it fills my whole being and smothers all other feelings. I don't know what sadness, grief, or boredom is. Here I am not asleep; I suffer from sleeplessness, but I am not dull. I say it in earnest; I begin to feel perplexed."
T291	Entity 48208 48210	he
T292	Cue 48268 48275	talking
T293	Entity 48279 48287	the monk
R352	Speaker Arg1:T291 Arg2:T290	
R353	Cueing Arg1:T292 Arg2:T290	
R354	Addressee Arg1:T293 Arg2:T290	
T294	Quotation 48827 48837	"But why?"
T295	Entity 48838 48846	the monk
T296	Cue 48847 48852	asked
T297	Quotation 48864 49214	"Is joy a supernatural feeling? Ought it not to be the normal state of man? The more highly a man is developed on the intellectual and moral side, the more independent he is, the more pleasure life gives him. Socrates, Diogenes, and Marcus Aurelius, were joyful, not sorrowful. And the Apostle tells us: 'Rejoice continually'; 'Rejoice and be glad.'"
R355	Speaker Arg1:T295 Arg2:T294	
R356	Speaker Arg1:T295 Arg2:T297	
R357	Cueing Arg1:T296 Arg2:T294	
R358	Cueing Arg1:T296 Arg2:T297	
T298	Quotation 49215 49256	"But will the gods be suddenly wrathful?"
T299	Entity 49257 49263	Kovrin
R359	Addressee Arg1:T299 Arg2:T294	
R360	Addressee Arg1:T299 Arg2:T297	
T300	Cue 49264 49270	jested
R361	Speaker Arg1:T299 Arg2:T298	
R362	Cueing Arg1:T300 Arg2:T298	
R363	Addressee Arg1:T295 Arg2:T298	
T301	Quotation 49288 49385	"If they take from me comfort and make me go cold and hungry, it won't be very much to my taste."
R364	Cueing Arg1:T300 Arg2:T301	
R365	Speaker Arg1:T299 Arg2:T301	
R366	Addressee Arg1:T295 Arg2:T301	
T302	Quotation 49603 49640	"Andryusha, whom are you talking to?"
T303	Entity 49641 49644	she
T304	Cue 49645 49650	asked
T305	Entity 49671 49673	he
R367	Speaker Arg1:T303 Arg2:T302	
R368	Cueing Arg1:T304 Arg2:T302	
R369	Addressee Arg1:T305 Arg2:T302	
T306	Quotation 49701 49719	"Andryusha! Whom?"
R370	Speaker Arg1:T303 Arg2:T306	
R371	Cueing Arg1:T304 Arg2:T306	
R372	Addressee Arg1:T305 Arg2:T306	
T307	Quotation 49720 49731	"Oh! Whom?"
T308	Cue 49732 49736	said
T309	Entity 49737 49743	Kovrin
R373	Cueing Arg1:T308 Arg2:T307	
R374	Speaker Arg1:T309 Arg2:T307	
R375	Addressee Arg1:T303 Arg2:T307	
T310	Quotation 49758 49798	"Why, to him. . . . He is sitting here,"
T311	Entity 49799 49801	he
T312	Cue 49802 49806	said
R376	Speaker Arg1:T311 Arg2:T310	
R377	Cueing Arg1:T312 Arg2:T310	
R378	Addressee Arg1:T303 Arg2:T310	
T313	Quotation 49836 49896	"There is no one here . . . no one! Andryusha, you are ill!"
T314	Entity 49897 49902	Tanya
R379	Speaker Arg1:T314 Arg2:T313	
T315	Entity 49921 49932	her husband
R380	Addressee Arg1:T315 Arg2:T313	
T316	Quotation 50031 50045	"You are ill!"
T317	Entity 50046 50049	she
